  "assessment": {
    "rationale": "A peer can record exact envelope bytes and the pCID locally, but this draft specimen only provides protocol-level scope and pushes actual naming semantics upward. That avoids turning the envelope into a central naming registry and keeps the layer boundary clear, yet it does not itself resolve Alice/Bob collisions for \"release\" or bind a name claim to an author. Deterministic CBOR and explicit unsupported/unverified unknown-pCID handling help, but draft unsigned v0 leaves weak long-term naming evidence.",
    "strengths": [
      "Protocol-level scope via pCID prevents different naming schemes from colliding at dispatch.",
      "Keeps human-readable naming out of the envelope, avoiding a built-in global registry.",
      "Peers can locally record exact deterministic-CBOR bytes.",
      "Unknown-pCID outputs are explicitly labeled unsupported and unverified."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "No rule scopes a shared name like \"release\" by publisher, site, group, or feed within one protocol.",
      "Unsigned v0 gives no envelope-level proof of who asserted a name mapping.",
      "Best-effort payload exposure can reveal name-like fields without authoritative interpretation."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "Upper layers may adopt incompatible scoping conventions and still appear wire-compatible.",
      "Spoofing, replay, and archive ambiguity remain hard to detect without signatures.",
      "Tooling may over-trust salvaged unknown-pCID payloads and treat ambiguous labels as real names."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
      "Which upper-layer protocol defines name scope and update semantics for this envelope family?",
      "How is long-term publisher attribution preserved if transport, not the envelope, supplies authenticity?",
      "Should unknown-pCID tooling hide or heavily warn on recovered human-readable labels?"
    ],
    "authority_boundary": "Evidence only; does not settle PromiseGrid design."
  }
}
